    heelIntro3: "Heels are particularly susceptible to drying out because they are in constant contact with the ground and bear the entire body weight. Extra attention is needed if you work standing for long periods or wear poorly ventilated shoes. It is important to let your feet breathe, which means wearing open shoes or going barefoot at home.",
    heelNutritionTitle: "The importance of nutrition and hydration for skin health",
    heelNutrition: "Skin health largely depends on nutrition and adequate fluid intake. You should drink enough water every day, approximately 15-30 milliliters per kilogram of body weight. Additionally, consider taking supplements that contain vitamins and antioxidants beneficial for the skin, such as vitamins A, E and C, as well as omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acids.",
    heelRemedyTitle: "Effective remedy for dry heels",
    heelRemedy1: "Urea-containing creams are an excellent remedy for caring for dry and cracked heels. For mild dryness problems, a cream containing 5-10% urea is sufficient, but for more severe cases when heels are already rough and hard, a cream with higher 30-50% urea content should be preferred. Urea helps deeply moisturize the skin and prevent cracks from deepening.",
    heelRemedy2: "In addition to moisturizing, it is important to regularly wash your feet, preferably with soap-free cleansers that do not dry out the skin. Dry your feet carefully and apply softening and moisturizing foot creams every evening containing ingredients such as urea, shea butter, menthol, horse chestnut, protease, allantoin, and other active ingredients.",
    heelWhenTitle: "When to see a specialist?",
    heelWhen: "If home care products do not produce the desired results or the infection worsens, it is advisable to see a specialist. Pharmacists and foot care specialists can advise on the best products and treatment options. If over-the-counter products don't help, you may need the assistance of a podologist.",

    fungusTitle: "Foot fungus: causes, symptoms and prevention",
    fungusCausesTitle: "Causes of foot fungus and its prevention",
    fungusCausesIntro: "There are many reasons for foot fungus development that can promote fungal infection. Most people encounter it during their lifetime, but fortunately it can be prevented by understanding risk factors and taking timely measures.",
    fungusCause1: "<strong>Excessive sweating</strong> – when feet sweat, remain moist and don't get enough air, the environment becomes favorable for fungal infection development.",
    fungusCause2: "<strong>Inadequate drying of feet after washing or swimming</strong> – moisture on the feet is an ideal breeding ground for fungal infections.",
    fungusCause3: "<strong>Wearing tight shoes</strong> – feet are compressed, cannot breathe, moisture accumulates in the shoes and contributes to nail trauma and fungal diseases.",
    fungusCause4: "<strong>Walking barefoot in public places</strong> – gyms, pools and spas are places where fungal infections often spread.",
    fungusSymTitle: "Common foot fungus symptoms:",
    fungusSym1: "Skin begins to redden and flake",
    fungusSym2: "Itching and burning sensation",
    fungusSym3: "Blisters and unpleasant odor",
    fungusSym4: "If left untreated, the infection can spread to toenails, causing thickening, brittleness and discoloration.",
    fungusPreventTitle: "How to prevent foot fungus?",
    fungusPrevent: "Maintaining good foot hygiene is the first step in preventing foot fungus: daily washing and thorough drying. Leaving feet moist promotes fungal infection development. Wear breathable footwear. Choose materials that allow air circulation and avoid tight shoes. It is best to rotate shoes regularly so they can dry out. Keep your feet dry. If your feet tend to sweat, consider using special foot powders that keep the skin dry and reduce the chance of fungal infections.",
    fungusRemedyTitle: "Home remedies for foot fungus treatment",
    fungusRemedyIntro: "If foot fungus has already developed, several home remedies can be used:",
    fungusRemedy1: "<strong>Tea tree oil and garlic</strong> – these natural ingredients have antifungal properties and have been used for centuries.",
    fungusRemedy2: "<strong>Sea salt bath</strong> – soaking feet in warm water with sea salt can help alleviate symptoms.",
    fungusRemedy3: "<strong>Essential oils</strong> – lavender, peppermint and eucalyptus are known for their antifungal and antibacterial properties.",
    fungusWhenTitle: "When to see a foot care specialist?",
    fungusWhen: "If home remedies don't produce results or the infection worsens, it is important to see a healthcare professional. Foot fungus and nail fungus are common concerns that there is no reason to be embarrassed about – specialists can help you with appropriate treatment.",
